## Working With Count Data: `summarize()`

If going to put into the `chisq.test()` function, want to pivot and get rid of additional columns.

Many ways to do this, but one example:

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
chisqdat <- untallied_admissions_data |>
  count(college, year) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = college,
              values_from = n) |>
  select(-year)
chisqdat
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 3 × 6
  `College of Computing` `College of Design` `College of Engineering`
                   <int>               <int>                    <int>
1                   1533                 287                     3584
2                   1265                 282                     3570
3                   1310                 313                     3795
# ℹ 3 more variables: `College of Sciences` <int>, `Ivan Allen College` <int>,
#   `Scheller College of Business` <int>
```


:::
:::

:::

::: {.column width="50%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
chisq.test(chisqdat)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

	Pearson's Chi-squared test

data:  chisqdat
X-squared = 36.938, df = 10, p-value = 5.801e-05
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
chisq.test(chisqdat, correct = F)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

	Pearson's Chi-squared test

data:  chisqdat
X-squared = 36.938, df = 10, p-value = 5.801e-05
```


